    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "We will design a numerical experiment to test the hypothesis that the solution space of analytic constraints is contained in a low-dimensional linear subspace, making the minimal network width $N_c(w)$ constant (geometric rigidity) rather than growing with the number of constraints $w$. We will define a family of analytic constraints (e.g., polynomial or trigonometric) that define a low-dimensional manifold, then systematically scan network widths $N$ and constraint counts $w$. For each $(w, N)$ pair, we train a small feedforward network to satisfy the constraints on training points and evaluate the maximum absolute error on held-out test points. We will identify $N_c(w)$ as the smallest $N$ where the test error $\\le 10^{-3}$. We will then analyze the trend of $N_c(w)$ versus $w$: if $N_c(w)$ remains roughly constant up to the saturation point of the subspace, the hypothesis is supported (rigid); if $N_c(w)$ grows indefinitely or fails to reach zero violation within practical widths, the solution space is \"fat\". We will report the scan parameters, training/test errors, and computed $N_c(w)$ values."
    },
